# Practical Manual

## Lab 02.2: Role-Play — A 10-Minute Sprint Planning

---

## Lab Overview

This **lab** is a hands-on simulation of Sprint Planning, the first of the five Scrum events. Working in small groups, you will take on Scrum accountabilities, negotiate a Sprint Goal from a ready-made Product Backlog, and forecast a realistic slice of work for a one-week Sprint — all inside a tight, realistic time box.

The exercise deliberately compresses a real Sprint Planning session (which the Scrum Guide time-boxes at a maximum of **eight hours for a one-month Sprint**, scaled down for shorter Sprints) into a few minutes, so you feel the discipline of time-boxing and forecasting under pressure, not just read about it.

**Format:** Simulation, in groups of 4.
**Total time:** 25 minutes activity + 5–10 minutes whole-class debrief.

---

## Learning Outcomes

By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

- Distinguish a **Sprint Goal** (why the Sprint matters) from a simple **list of selected backlog items** (what will be built).
- Practise the core exchange of Sprint Planning: the Product Owner proposing *what* and *why*, and the Developers deciding *how much* and *how*.
- Produce a one-sentence **plan** for a single Product Backlog item, demonstrating the difference between forecasting and detailed task breakdown.
- Critically evaluate whether a Sprint Goal explains **why the work matters**, rather than being a restated to-do list.

---

## Prerequisites

**Before beginning, make sure you can confidently explain:**

- The purpose and time-box of **Sprint Planning** (Lesson 2, section 2.4).
- The difference between the **Product Backlog** and the **Sprint Backlog**.
- What a good **Sprint Goal** looks like, as distinct from a list of tickets.
- The accountabilities of **Product Owner**, **Scrum Master**, and **Developers**.

**You will need:**

- Groups of **4 people**.
- One printed or shared copy per group of the **Mini Product Backlog** (provided in full below — no separate download needed).
- A timer visible to the whole room (the facilitator's, or each group's own phone).
- A shared document, whiteboard, or sticky notes for each group to record its Sprint Goal and forecast.

---

## Role Assignments (2 minutes)

Each group of 4 assigns:

| Role | Count | Responsibility in this exercise |
|---|---|---|
| **Product Owner (PO)** | 1 | Knows the Product Goal and the priority order of the backlog; explains *why* each item matters; answers Developers' clarifying questions; does **not** decide *how* the work gets done |
| **Scrum Master** | 1 | Keeps time; keeps the group focused on producing a *Sprint Goal*, not just a list; gently interrupts if the group starts designing a technical solution in detail (that belongs later, not in Planning) |
| **Developers** | 2 | Ask questions to understand the *why*; decide which items are realistically achievable in the Sprint; forecast the work; sketch a one-sentence plan for the top item |

If your group has 3 or 5 people, double up on Developers or Product Owner support rather than skip a role.

---

## Materials: The Mini Product Backlog

Below is a ready-to-use Product Backlog of 8 items for a fictional **"Campus Events App"** — a mobile app that helps students discover, RSVP to, and get reminders about on-campus events (societies, sports, careers fairs, open lectures).

**Product Goal:** *Help every student find at least one event a month that they would not otherwise have known about, and reduce no-shows at university-run events.*

The Product Owner should read the Product Goal aloud to the group before revealing the backlog table.

| # | Backlog Item | Priority | Rough size |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | As a student, I can browse a list of upcoming campus events, filtered by category (sports, careers, societies, academic) | Highest | Medium |
| 2 | As a student, I can RSVP to an event and receive a reminder notification 1 hour before it starts | High | Medium |
| 3 | As an event organiser, I can create and publish a new event with a title, description, date, time and location | High | Medium |
| 4 | As a student, I can see an event's location on a campus map | Medium | Large |
| 5 | As a student, I can add an event directly to my phone's calendar | Medium | Small |
| 6 | As a student, I can see which of my friends have RSVP'd to the same event | Low | Large |
| 7 | As an event organiser, I can see a simple count of RSVPs for my event | Medium | Small |
| 8 | As a student, I can search events by keyword | Low | Small |

*(Note for facilitators: item sizes are deliberately rough and unitless — "Small / Medium / Large" — since teaching formal estimation techniques is covered in Lesson 8. Groups should reason about relative size only, not produce story points.)*

