## Facilitator Notes (optional — for tutors running this session)

<details>
<summary>Click to expand facilitator guidance</summary>

- **Timing is the teaching tool here.** Do not extend the 25 minutes even if groups are still debating — running out of time and having to forecast anyway is a deliberate, realistic feature of the exercise, not a flaw.
- **Most common failure mode:** the "Sprint Goal" is really just item 1 and item 2's titles glued together. Use the debrief to contrast this with a genuine outcome-based goal.
- **Second most common failure mode:** the PO starts dictating implementation details in Step 2. Gently redirect — this is exactly the anti-pattern the exercise is designed to surface.
- **Room setup:** groups of 4 work best seated together with one shared surface (laptop, tablet, or paper) visible to all four roles.
- **Extension activity for fast groups:** ask them to identify which backlog item they would cut first if, mid-Sprint, item 1 turned out to be twice as complex as expected — and why.
- **Scaling for larger classes:** this activity works unchanged for any number of groups of 4; simply extend the whole-class debrief slightly to sample 3–4 groups rather than all of them.

</details>

